In computer science, data is represented in different forms so that computers can store, process, transmit, and understand it. Since you're studying binary manipulation and offensive security, you should understand these representations because applications, operating systems, network protocols, and executable files all depend on them.

Below is a comprehensive map of the major ways data is represented.

### 1\. Number representations

These represent numerical values inside computers.

| Representation | Base | Symbols | Example | |---|---:|---|---| | Binary | 2 | 0, 1 | `1100` | | Octal | 8 | 0–7 | `14` | | Decimal | 10 | 0–9 | `12` | | Hexadecimal | 16 | 0–9, A–F | `0x0C` | | Unary | 1 | Repeated marks | `||||` |

Other important numeric representations:

* Unsigned integers

* Signed integers

* Sign-and-magnitude

* One's complement

* Two's complement

* Fixed-point numbers

* Floating-point numbers

* IEEE 754 single precision (32-bit)

* IEEE 754 double precision (64-bit)

* Binary-coded decimal (BCD)

* Gray code

* Excess-K representation

* Variable-length integers (Varints)

### 2\. Text and character representations

These represent letters, numbers, punctuation and symbols.

| Representation | Purpose                                     |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| ASCII          | Basic English characters                    |
| Extended ASCII | Extended 8-bit character sets               |
| Unicode        | Universal character repertoire              |
| UTF-8          | Variable-length Unicode encoding            |
| UTF-16         | Unicode encoding using 16-bit code units    |
| UTF-32         | Unicode encoding using 32-bit code units    |
| UTF-7          | Older Unicode encoding                      |
| EBCDIC         | Character encoding used in some IBM systems |
| ISO-8859       | Family of 8-bit character encodings         |
| Windows-1252   | Western European character encoding         |
| ISCII          | Indian-script encoding family               |
| Shift JIS      | Japanese character encoding                 |
| GBK            | Chinese character encoding                  |
| Big5           | Traditional Chinese character encoding      |

Example:

```python
text = "A"

print(ord(text))       # 65
print(bin(ord(text)))  # 0b1000001
print(text.encode())   # b'A'
print(text.encode().hex())  # 41
```

